          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which histological pattern in the cervix is virtually pathognomonic for Chlamydi</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which histological pattern in the cervix is virtually pathognomonic for Chlamydia trachomatis infection?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Neutrophilic infiltrate with mucopurulent discharge</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A neutrophilic infiltrate indicates acute cervicitis, which can be caused by gonorrhoea, Chlamydia, or HSV-2 — it is not specific to any single organism.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nabothian cysts with a lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Nabothian cysts with lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate characterise chronic cervicitis in general and are not specific to Chlamydia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lymphoid follicles within the cervical stroma</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! Follicular cervicitis — lymphoid follicles forming in the cervical stroma — is virtually pathognomonic for Chlamydia trachomatis, even when the infection is clinically silent.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Squamous metaplasia in the transformation zone</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Squamous metaplasia in the transformation zone is a normal physiological adaptation and not a marker of any specific infection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Follicular cervicitis, defined by lymphoid follicles in the cervical stroma, is virtually pathognomonic for Chlamydia trachomatis. Unlike the non-specific neutrophilic infiltrate of acute cervicitis, this pattern points specifically to Chlamydia and warrants targeted treatment."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Follicular cervicitis — lymphoid follicles in the stroma — is the histological signature of Chlamydia trachomatis. Recognising this pattern is important because Chlamydia infection in the transformation zone is often clinically silent yet can ascend to cause tubal infertility."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
