          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which molecular event is the earliest and most frequently detected change in aty</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which molecular event is the earliest and most frequently detected change in atypical endometrial hyperplasia?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>TP53 mutation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>TP53 mutation drives Type II serous endometrial carcinoma, which arises in atrophic endometrium — a completely separate pathway from the hyperplasia-to-Type I sequence.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>KRAS mutation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>KRAS mutation contributes to the Type I carcinoma molecular signature but appears alongside PTEN loss, not before it in the progression sequence.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Microsatellite instability</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Microsatellite instability is part of the Type I molecular profile but is not the earliest event. PTEN loss precedes it in the hyperplasia–carcinoma sequence.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>PTEN tumour suppressor loss</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! PTEN loss is the earliest molecular event in atypical endometrial hyperplasia, detected in over 80% of cases, and initiates the pathway to Type I endometrioid carcinoma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "PTEN loss is the earliest molecular event, seen in over 80% of atypical hyperplasia cases. It initiates the Type I pathway (PTEN loss → microsatellite instability → KRAS mutation). TP53 mutation, by contrast, drives the separate Type II serous carcinoma pathway arising in atrophic endometrium."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "PTEN tumour suppressor loss is the gateway event in the hyperplasia–carcinoma sequence. Found in over 80% of atypical hyperplasia cases, it drives the progression to Type I endometrioid carcinoma, which accounts for roughly 80% of all endometrial cancers."
                }
              ],
