          "label": "<p>Which ovarian tumour is identified by markedly elevated serum alpha-fetoprotein </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which ovarian tumour is identified by markedly elevated serum alpha-fetoprotein and Schiller-Duval bodies — glomeruloid structures around a central blood vessel — on histology?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Dysgerminoma</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Dysgerminoma is marked by elevated placental alkaline phosphatase and LDH, not alpha-fetoprotein. It is the ovarian counterpart of the testicular seminoma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ovarian choriocarcinoma</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Ovarian choriocarcinoma elevates β-hCG, not alpha-fetoprotein, and shows a biphasic cytotrophoblast–syncytiotrophoblast pattern without glomeruloid structures.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Yolk sac tumour</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! The yolk sac tumour produces alpha-fetoprotein and shows Schiller-Duval bodies — glomeruloid structures surrounding a central vessel. These two features together are diagnostic.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Granulosa cell tumour</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Granulosa cell tumours secrete oestrogen and show Call-Exner bodies with inhibin positivity. They do not produce alpha-fetoprotein.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The yolk sac tumour produces alpha-fetoprotein and shows Schiller-Duval bodies — glomeruloid structures around a central vessel. Remember each tumour's unique marker–morphology pair: dysgerminoma has PLAP and LDH, choriocarcinoma has β-hCG with biphasic trophoblasts, and granulosa cell tumour has oestrogen with Call-Exner bodies."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The yolk sac tumour is defined by markedly elevated alpha-fetoprotein and Schiller-Duval bodies on histology. Each ovarian tumour has a characteristic marker–morphology pair: PLAP/LDH for dysgerminoma, β-hCG for choriocarcinoma, and inhibin for granulosa cell tumour."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
