          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A histology slide shows glandular structures invading the myometrium, surrounded</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A histology slide shows glandular structures invading the myometrium, surrounded by desmoplastic stroma, with back-to-back glands exhibiting nuclear atypia. Which diagnosis does this combination of features establish?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Endometrial hyperplasia without atypia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hyperplasia without atypia shows crowded glands but lacks nuclear atypia, desmoplastic stroma, and true myometrial invasion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Adenomyosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Adenomyosis places benign endometrial glands within the myometrium, but the glands lack nuclear atypia and the desmoplastic stromal response that signals true invasion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Leiomyosarcoma</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Leiomyosarcoma arises from smooth muscle, not glandular epithelium. It is identified by spindle cell atypia, a high mitotic count, and coagulative necrosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Endometrial carcinoma</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! The combination of myometrial invasion with desmoplastic stroma and back-to-back atypical glands establishes endometrial carcinoma. Desmoplasia is the key feature distinguishing true invasion from adenomyosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Endometrial carcinoma is identified by glandular invasion of the myometrium with a desmoplastic stromal response and nuclear atypia. Without desmoplasia, similar-looking glands in the myometrium would suggest adenomyosis. Without atypia, crowded glands suggest hyperplasia. Apply the four-step lens: gross, architecture, cell detail, then clinical correlate."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Myometrial invasion with desmoplastic stroma and atypical back-to-back glands confirms endometrial carcinoma. Desmoplasia distinguishes true malignant invasion from the benign glandular displacement of adenomyosis — a critical distinction at the microscope."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
